00:03:39.633 --> 00:03:43.412
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

67
00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.469
Visal, what is one financial habit you want to develop?

68
00:03:48.583 --> 00:03:52.803
Saving every week. I used to spend without a budget.

69
00:03:53.917 --> 00:03:55.162
What did you buy?

70
00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:00.655
I would buy snacks and games without thinking. I never saved anything.

71
00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:02.565
And now?

72
00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:07.890
Now I use a budgeting app, and I'm building an emergency fund.

73
00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:10.545
Part three: grammar.

74
00:04:11.383 --> 00:04:14.799
Grammar focus: used to vs would (past habits).

75
00:04:15.099 --> 00:04:16.333
Look at the pattern.

76
00:04:17.017 --> 00:04:19.016
I used to spend without a budget.

77
00:04:19.917 --> 00:04:21.603
I would spend impulsively.

78
00:04:22.517 --> 00:04:24.342
I used to have no savings.

79
00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:32.016
Use 'used to' for past habits and states. Use 'would' only for repeated past actions, not for states like 'have' or 'be'.

80
00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:36.598
Be careful! Don't say: I used to spending without a budget.

81
00:04:38.533 --> 00:04:41.183
Say: I used to spend without a budget.

82
00:04:41.483 --> 00:04:44.098
After 'used to', use the base verb.

83
00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:47.413
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

84
00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.619
My Money Story.

85
00:04:49.919 --> 00:04:52.592
I used to spend all my pocket money in one day.

86
00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:55.553
I would buy snacks without thinking.

87
00:04:56.567 --> 00:05:00.610
Financial literacy taught me about budgeting apps and emergency funds.

88
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.341
